---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const stages = [
  { n: '01', name: 'Input',
    body: 'The claim the AI is about to act on, with the context that qualifies it: where it came from, when, in what domain, and from which model.' },
  { n: '02', name: 'Encoding',
    body: 'Embedding generation, token normalisation and domain tagging, so the claim can be compared against a body of sources rather than against a prompt.' },
  { n: '03', name: 'Retrieval',
    body: 'Nearest-neighbour search over structured corpora, filtered by time, to gather the sources that bear on the claim.' },
  { n: '04', name: 'Independence and disagreement',
    body: 'Sources that agree because they share an origin are linked and discounted, so one claim repeated five times does not score as five confirmations. Outright disagreement between sources is detected and carried into the result instead of being averaged away.' },
  { n: '05', name: 'Enough support for the stakes',
    body: 'How much independent support a claim must carry before it may pass, scaled to what acting on it would cost. A cheap, reversible action needs less than an expensive, permanent one.' },
  { n: '06', name: 'Motion over time',
    body: 'Whether the way a claim moves through its sources over time (how fast it spreads, how its support shifts) is itself a useful warning sign. This is the research question at the center of Lane Vector, stated as a hypothesis with a way to be proven wrong.' },
  { n: '07', name: 'The result',
    body: 'A structured result the calling system reads: the decision, what supports it, what conflicts with it, the uncertainty, and the path taken to reach it.' }
];
---
<Base
  title="Technology — TruVector"
  description="How TruVector checks the information behind an AI action: the pipeline stage by stage, the result a system reads, and how it differs from permission-based AI gates."
  path="/technology"
>

  <section class="room" style="padding-top:96px;padding-bottom:56px" aria-labelledby="tech-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Technology</p>
      <h1 class="suite-title" id="tech-title" style="max-width:24ch">A checkpoint between what an AI read and what it does.</h1>
      <hr class="rule--champagne" style="margin-top:32px" />
      <div class="reveal plain" style="margin-top:28px;max-width:62ch">
        <p class="kicker" style="margin-bottom:10px">In plain terms</p>
        <p>Before an AI acts, TruVector checks where the information came from, whether the sources are really separate, and whether they agree. It answers Allow, Review or Block, with the reasons attached, so someone can check the call later. The rest of this page is the technical detail.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="room" id="pipeline" aria-labelledby="pipeline-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Pipeline</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="pipeline-title">Seven stages from claim to decision.</h2>
      <ul class="rail reveal" style="margin-top:48px">
        {stages.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <span class="rail__name"><span style="color:var(--stone-text);font-family:var(--label);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.1em;margin-right:12px">{s.n}</span>{s.name}</span>
            <span class="rail__note">{s.body}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room room--alt" id="artifact" aria-labelledby="artifact-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Output</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="artifact-title">What the calling system reads.</h2>
      <p class="reveal" style="margin-top:20px;max-width:60ch">The result is stable, versioned and designed to be logged, so that months later a reviewer can reconstruct not just what was decided but what it was decided on. A simplified example:</p>

      <div class="reveal artifact" style="margin-top:40px">
        <pre><code>{`{
  "decision":            "allow" | "review" | "block",
  "support_score":       float,
  "confidence_interval": [float, float],
  "supporting_sources":  [ ... ],
  "conflicting_sources": [ ... ],
  "independence":        { "shared_origin_discount": float },
  "decision_path":       [ ... ],
  "timestamp":           "ISO 8601"
}`}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="caption reveal" style="margin-top:20px;max-width:60ch">A consumer can always tell the difference between &ldquo;no disagreement was found&rdquo; and &ldquo;disagreement was not checked.&rdquo;</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room" id="positioning" aria-labelledby="pos-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">How it differs</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="pos-title">How this differs from other AI gates.</h2>
      <div class="flagship reveal" style="margin-top:44px">
        <div class="flagship__pane">
          <p class="lede">Other tools also put a gate in front of AI actions. Most ask a permission question: <em>is this AI allowed to do this?</em> That&rsquo;s a yes/no lookup against a policy.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flagship__pane">
          <p>TruVector asks a different question: <em>is the information behind this action solid enough for what the action would cost?</em> That&rsquo;s a call made under uncertainty, so it comes with a stated margin of error.</p>
          <p>The two checks work side by side. Neither replaces the other.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room room--alt" id="operating" aria-labelledby="op-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Operating profile</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="op-title">What running it looks like.</h2>
      <div class="tracks reveal">
        <div class="track">
          <h3 class="room-title">Workload shape</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>API-driven, request-scoped, no long-running state</li>
            <li>Retrieval-intensive; embedding generation bounded and cacheable</li>
            <li>Latency-sensitive, because it sits in front of an action</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="track">
          <h3 class="room-title">Cost discipline</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Embeddings reused rather than regenerated</li>
            <li>Retrieval cached where the corpus is stable</li>
            <li>Ingestion cadence controlled rather than continuous</li>
            <li>Measured as cost per check, so scale stays legible</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room" aria-labelledby="tech-cta">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="tech-cta" style="font-size:1.6rem">The technical brief goes further than this page.</h2>
      <p class="reveal" style="margin-top:16px;max-width:56ch">Test protocol, corpus construction, calibration method, and how each hypothesis could be proven wrong. Sent on request.</p>
      <div class="actions reveal" style="margin-top:32px">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/investors#request">Request the technical brief</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  .plain {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--bronze-edge);
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  .artifact {
    border: 1px solid var(--bronze-edge);
    background: var(--smoked-bronze);
    border-radius: 1px;
    padding: 24px;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .artifact pre { margin: 0; }
  .artifact code {
    font-family: var(--label);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--parchment);
    white-space: pre;
  }
</style>
